{"name":"Flip-Flop","hex":"#f2c4a7","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"flip-flop","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A sandal consisting of a rubber sole fastened to the foot by a rubber thong fitting between the toes and around the sides of the foot. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","A change of places; an inversion or swap."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To alternate back and forth between directly opposite opinions, ideas, or decisions."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/flip-flop"}],"entries":[{"title":"Flip-Flop (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Flip-Flop is the fourth and final studio album by the American band Guadalcanal Diary. It was released in 1989 on Elektra Records. The band supported the album with a North American tour. \"Always Saturday\", for which a video was shot, was released as a single."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/59/GDFlipFlop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip-Flop_(album)","wikidata":"Q5459632"},{"title":"Flip-flop (electronics)","blocks":[{"text":"In electronics, flip-flops and latches are circuits that have two stable states that can store state information – a bistable multivibrator. The circuit can be made to change state by signals applied to one or more control inputs and will output its state. It is the basic storage element in sequential logic. Flip-flops and latches are fundamental building blocks of digital electronics systems used in computers, communications, and many other types of systems."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Transistor_Bistable_interactive_animated-en.svg/330px-Transistor_Bistable_interactive_animated-en.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flip-flop_(electronics)","wikidata":"Q183406"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/59/GDFlipFlop.jpg/250px-GDFlipFlop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/59/GDFlipFlop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/59/GDFlipFlop.jpg/250px-GDFlipFlop.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGDFlipFlop.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Eccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png/500px-Eccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Eccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png/1280px-Eccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/98/Eccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png/500px-Eccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Schematics from the Eccles and Jordan trigger relay patent filed 1918, one drawn as a cascade of amplifiers with a positive feedback path, and the other as a symmetric cross-coupled pair","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEccles-Jordan_trigger_circuit_flip-flip_drawings.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/R-S_mk2.gif/330px-R-S_mk2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/R-S_mk2.gif/330px-R-S_mk2.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An animation of an SR latch, constructed from a pair of cross-coupled NOR gates. Red and black mean logical '1' and '0', respectively.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AR-S_mk2.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/SRLatch-lowres.gif/250px-SRLatch-lowres.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8f/SRLatch-lowres.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/SRLatch-lowres.gif/250px-SRLatch-lowres.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An animated SR latch. Black and white mean logical '1' and '0', respectively.S = 1, R = 0: SetS = 0, R = 0: HoldS = 0, R = 1: ResetS = 1, R = 1: Not allowed\nTransitioning from the restricted combination (D) to (A) leads to an unstable state.\n","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASRLatch-lowres.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/SR_NOR_Latch_How_to_Work_Ver2_20251008.png/500px-SR_NOR_Latch_How_to_Work_Ver2_20251008.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/87/SR_NOR_Latch_How_to_Work_Ver2_20251008.png/500px-SR_NOR_Latch_How_to_Work_Ver2_20251008.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"How an SR NOR latch works","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASR_NOR_Latch_How_to_Work_Ver2_20251008.png"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Inverted_SR_latch_symbol.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Inverted_SR_latch_symbol.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"Symbol for an SR NAND latch","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInverted_SR_latch_symbol.png"},{"srcset":"https://th